WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.630 --> 00:00:04.340
- Welcome to Introduction to Economics.

00:00:04.340 --> 00:00:08.340
You are about to become an
economically-literate person.

00:00:08.340 --> 00:00:10.300
You might not realize
this, but you've always

00:00:10.300 --> 00:00:12.280
been an economic actor.

00:00:12.280 --> 00:00:15.460
When you've decided to spend
your time doing one thing,

00:00:15.460 --> 00:00:18.340
you might have foregone being
able to do other things,

00:00:18.340 --> 00:00:20.190
that's an opportunity cost.

00:00:20.190 --> 00:00:23.020
When you decided to buy something,

00:00:23.020 --> 00:00:25.940
you might ask yourself,
why did I pay that price?

00:00:25.940 --> 00:00:28.840
Would I pay more or less if
there were more of that thing?

00:00:28.840 --> 00:00:30.700
Would I pay more or less
if there were more mes

00:00:30.700 --> 00:00:32.190
who wanted to buy that thing?

00:00:32.190 --> 00:00:33.190
All of a sudden, you're getting

00:00:33.190 --> 00:00:35.860
into the ideas of supply and demand.

00:00:35.860 --> 00:00:37.560
As we go into these lessons, we're going

00:00:37.560 --> 00:00:39.570
to dig a little bit deeper at a little bit

00:00:39.570 --> 00:00:41.780
of math, a little bit of visualizations,

00:00:41.780 --> 00:00:44.740
where we'll see that these
simple but very powerful

00:00:44.740 --> 00:00:49.740
frameworks apply to things
as complex as economies.

00:00:49.960 --> 00:00:53.180
The entire academic field of economics

00:00:53.180 --> 00:00:56.300
is studying how things should be allocated

00:00:56.300 --> 00:00:58.210
when there's not enough for everyone

00:00:58.210 --> 00:01:00.660
to get as much as they want,
that's known as scarcity.

00:01:00.660 --> 00:01:03.230
How do you allocate scarce resources?

00:01:03.230 --> 00:01:05.870
And to do that, you
have to make trade-offs.

00:01:05.870 --> 00:01:08.650
We're also going to learn
about concepts of trade.

00:01:08.650 --> 00:01:09.920
Why is trade good?

00:01:09.920 --> 00:01:11.720
Ideas like comparative advantage,

00:01:11.720 --> 00:01:13.790
but also why trade might not always

00:01:13.790 --> 00:01:15.530
be in everyone's interest.

00:01:15.530 --> 00:01:17.850
And as we go into these
and we study things

00:01:17.850 --> 00:01:20.850
like supply and demand,
perhaps most importantly,

00:01:20.850 --> 00:01:23.740
we're gonna start to learn
to think like an economist,

00:01:23.740 --> 00:01:25.240
where instead of just
thinking about things

00:01:25.240 --> 00:01:28.230
like prices and scarcity,
and supply and demand,

00:01:28.230 --> 00:01:29.690
and everyday language, we're gonna think

00:01:29.690 --> 00:01:30.970
about how do we model them?

00:01:30.970 --> 00:01:32.560
Because once we can model them and think

00:01:32.560 --> 00:01:34.430
about them visually and mathematically,

00:01:34.430 --> 00:01:38.090
we can come to interesting
and rigorous conclusions.

00:01:38.090 --> 00:01:41.130
Economics is sometimes
called the dismal science,

00:01:41.130 --> 00:01:43.270
which some might view
as a bit of an insult

00:01:43.270 --> 00:01:45.380
to the field, but I
view it as a compliment,

00:01:45.380 --> 00:01:47.040
because what's interesting
about economics,

00:01:47.040 --> 00:01:48.940
it has some traits that
make it a little bit more

00:01:48.940 --> 00:01:50.430
like traditional sciences.

00:01:50.430 --> 00:01:52.020
We're going to use some mathematics,

00:01:52.020 --> 00:01:53.840
we're going to do some
diagrams, we're going

00:01:53.840 --> 00:01:56.100
to be thinking
systematically about things.

00:01:56.100 --> 00:01:58.430
But it has another aspect of it

00:01:58.430 --> 00:02:00.260
that is a lot like the humanities.

00:02:00.260 --> 00:02:03.160
We're gonna talk about how humans act,

00:02:03.160 --> 00:02:05.560
and especially how they act in aggregate.

00:02:05.560 --> 00:02:07.110
Even as we look at these models

00:02:07.110 --> 00:02:09.270
and we try to understand
things mathematically,

00:02:09.270 --> 00:02:11.130
we're always going to be asking ourselves,

00:02:11.130 --> 00:02:13.370
well what assumptions
did we make to do that?

00:02:13.370 --> 00:02:15.780
Is that true, are all humans rational

00:02:15.780 --> 00:02:17.230
when they make these decisions?

00:02:17.230 --> 00:02:19.156
Or are there animal
spirits that get worked

00:02:19.156 --> 00:02:21.530
up where we sometimes
do irrational things,

00:02:21.530 --> 00:02:22.960
where as a group, it actually could

00:02:22.960 --> 00:02:24.270
be a little less predictable

00:02:24.270 --> 00:02:27.030
than the mathematical models predict.

00:02:27.030 --> 00:02:30.110
So this is going to be a fun
beginning to our journey.

00:02:30.110 --> 00:02:32.640
And like always, if you're interested

00:02:32.640 --> 00:02:34.660
in this topic and you want to go deeper

00:02:34.660 --> 00:02:37.480
and you want to get practice
with immediate feedback,

00:02:37.480 --> 00:02:41.513
you could look at all of these
lessons at khanacademy.org.

